OxC3 web frontend, outstanding work
===================================

What is left to do on shader.oxsomi.com and the compiler paths behind it. STATUS.md holds the
capability table and the wider roadmap; this file is the working list for the frontend, kept at the
level of "what would someone pick up next".

An item leaves this list by landing WITH its tests and its documentation, per FOR_CONTRIBUTORS.md.


Before this goes out
--------------------

- web/* still needs a manual read-through by Niels. Nothing in it has had a human pass end to end.
- It needs an Astra review as well.


Next big thing
--------------

- Live AMD ISA in the browser, through a spirv2isa Mesa fork compiled to wasm. This is the last
  greyed-out capability on the page: the ISA tab works on desktop through amdllpc + amdgpu-dis, and
  in the browser it refuses because those cannot be spawned. Marked in STATUS.md as needing the fork.


Editor intelligence
-------------------

- Let the IntelliSense binary dropdown drive the selected binary, so the SPIR-V and DXIL views follow
  whichever permutation the editor is on. The catch is a recompile whose entrypoints no longer match
  the previous one's; that case needs a stale marker near the compile button rather than a silent
  mismatch.

- HLSL builtin intrinsics in hover and completion, with real signatures and a short description.
  Today hover resolves symbols the reflector emits, so a user's own declarations work and every
  builtin (mul, saturate, InterlockedAdd) is silent, which reads as a gap rather than a boundary.
- Doc comments: carry the comment above a declaration through the reflector into the hover card.
- Longer term, gated on a DXC IntelliSense seam: signature help while typing, an outline view, and
  rename / find-references.


Compiler and CLI, as the page uses them
---------------------------------------

- Extension x model validation table (for example PAQ below its minimum shader model), shared by the
  CLI and the web assemble card, so an illegal combination greys out instead of failing late.
- `shader assemble` into a real oiSH (an SHFile built from a binary plus an identifier). The web
  round-trip check would then refuse rather than warn.
- Reflect a bare .spv / .dxil has no CLI verb yet, though the page does it. The web CLI map says so.
- Parse-only permutation listing, so the page's "IntelliSense follows" picker can fill before any
  compile. Compiler_parse already yields the SHEntryRuntime this needs.
- Descriptor layout overrides in the pipeline editor: the page shows the derived oiPL read only, and
  editing a row and supplying one is missing.
- The page derives layouts with no excluded registers, so the runtime's own registers (the bindless
  arrays, the per frame globals) show up as rows the device would own. The CLI's exclusion list should
  reach the module's derive call.
- A visitor pasting an older shader that indexes samplers dynamically sees "undeclared identifier
  samplerUniform", because the bindless sampler array is opt in through
  [[oxc::extension("DynamicSamplers")]]; the diagnostic could say why.
- build_web.py --skip_host_package fails with conan's own "Unable to find oxc3/<version>" whenever the
  version moved and the cached host package is older; a hint naming --host_package_only would save a
  puzzled minute.
- docs/roadmap.md and the roadmap section of STATUS.md overlap; one should absorb the other, and this
  file should point at whichever survives.
- SBFile_combine mismatch errors should name the buffer and both sizes.
- coop_vec compiles to different DXIL from run to run. The same source, compiled repeatedly through the
  CLI, yields several distinct oiSH: the container hash moves and so do scattered bytes inside the DXIL
  bitcode, while SPIR-V stays stable and every other sample is stable on both backends. The sample
  suite therefore pins coop_vec's SPIR-V and skips its DXIL. Worth a reduced repro against the DXC fork
  the way the multi-base and new/delete ones were done, then re-enable the pin.
- No way to request a SPIR-V version. DXIL has the shader model (`[[oxc::model]]` and the CLI's
  `-model`), and SPIR-V has no equivalent, so a shader cannot say which SPIR-V version it targets and
  the toolchain's default is the only one available. The page's validator already reads the version
  out of a module's header, so the reader half exists; what is missing is the annotation, the CLI
  flag, the oiSH field to carry it, and passing it through to the SPIR-V backend.


JSON serialization belongs in the formats
-----------------------------------------

Each format writes its own JSON view through OxC3_formats_json (formats/json/json_writer.h) and
src/tools/oxc3_wasm only frames it. What is left is reaching those views from the CLI and widening them.

- `file data` could expose it under `--json`. That needs a pretty printer to be worth reading: oiSP's
  specializations come out as bare values today, which are neither readable nor editable.
- Under `--verbose`, DXIL and SPIR-V could carry a disassembly. In the long run that could replace the
  binary goldens, or sit alongside them.
- It might make sense to replace the normal print system with this, and to do the same for the other
  oi formats, oiDL and oiCA among them. Each one's shape is straightforward enough on its own.
- A deserializer is needed at some point too, not just a writer.


Reflection and the JSON views
-----------------------------

- Nodes should know their children when the AST is built. Emitting a node's child list currently scans
  every node looking for one whose parent is this node, which is quadratic in the node count and is by
  far the heaviest part of writing an oiSR document.
- The page always folds builtin includes in the symbol tree. Expanding them should be a switch; the
  document already carries every node with its builtin flag, so this is page work only.
- The JSON strings should reserve up front, so a document does not reallocate repeatedly while the
  first fields are written.
- The mock tier still invents four things: the regexes that guess which extensions a source uses, the
  SPIR-V capability names it prints per extension, the DXC argument line the Command tab reconstructs,
  and the wave-op model bumps. Each is a stand-in for something only the module can answer; the
  Command tab's is listed as not wired.
- Texture and StructuredBuffer inner type names: IHLSLReflection appears to give us the inner type,
  and hover does not show it.


Maintenance
-----------

- Split src/tools/oxc3_wasm/wasm_main.c into one file per export family; it holds every export in one
  file.
- Cap the size of an oiCA the page will accept, so a shared archive cannot flood the receiver's memory
  just because someone packed a large one. The number needs choosing; the check itself is a line.

- Split web/js/app.js into feature modules. Share and restore, the binary strip, and the tabs are the
  first three; new features already land as their own modules.
- UBSan (clang, -fsanitize=function) reports calls through mismatched function pointer types in the
  graphics C/C++ interop, because the oxc:: C++ twins of the C structs make the callback signatures
  distinct types. Harmless on current ABIs, but it is 25 reports of noise in every sanitizer run.


Parked
------

- Mouse back/forward navigating page state.
- Side-by-side against upstream DXC; an upstream binary is too large to fetch casually.
- GLSL and Slang input: HLSL only by design, stated in the docs.


Notes for whoever picks this up
-------------------------------

- This machine has no node or npm on PATH. The emsdk's node is the only one, and everything under
  web/dev needs it.
- The wasm bundle and the desktop build are two configurations of one toolchain. Alternating build.py
  and build_web.py needs no wipe, because the host tools get their own output tree through
  OxC3OutputTag. If a link ever shows "multiple definition" walls in graphics code nobody touched, a
  tree is being shared again and the tag is the fix.
- The DXC fork's dev tree and the three files that pin it are described in CLAUDE.md and the fork's
  own notes. Fork edits carry an `HLSL Change:` marker at most, matching upstream LLVM's sparseness.
